 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE WEST HIGHLAND WAY. Ninety-six miles from Milngavie, on the edge of Glasgow, to Fort William, and the first official long-distance path in Scotland, opened in 1980. It follows drove roads, military roads and old railway ground, so mostly somebody else has already done the hard thinking about where to put your feet.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_whw",
   "title": "The West Highland Way"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "RANNOCH MOOR. The railway crossed this moor in 1894. The ground was too soft for an ordinary embankment, so the engineers floated the track on mattresses of brushwood, earth and ash, and the trains have been running over a bog ever since. Nobody has been brave enough to ask it to stop.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_rannoch",
   "title": "Rannoch Moor"
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE BLACK MOUNT. Heather, rock and a great deal of sky. Red deer come down here in winter. In summer the midges do the same, in greater numbers, and with less dignity."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "DROVERS' ROAD. Cattle were walked south along this line to the markets for centuries. The drovers' dogs found their own way home, which is more than can be said for most walkers."
  }
 ],
